{% set design %}
<section class="docs-section">
  <h2 id="d-usage">Usage</h2>
  <p>The bottom-nav shell is the classic phone-first app frame: a top app bar,
     scrolling content, and a fixed bottom tab bar with 3–5 destinations.
     From 905px up, the same tabs become inline pills in a centered bar —
     no separate desktop navigation needed.</p>
  <h3>Do use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>You have <strong>3–5 top-level destinations</strong> — the sweet spot
        M2 recommends for bottom navigation.</li>
    <li>The app is mobile-primary and destinations should be one thumb-tap away.</li>
    <li>You want a FAB docked above the bar (the demo shows the pairing).</li>
  </ul>
  <h3>Don't use it when</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>There are more than five destinations — use the drawer or rail shell.</li>
    <li>Destinations need labels longer than a word or two — pills stretch,
        but the bar caps them.</li>
    <li>The page is a website rather than an app — tabs at the bottom read
        as mobile chrome, not web navigation.</li>
  </ul>
  {{ callout({
    variant: "info",
    title: "Same markup, two layouts",
    text: "The tab bar element is identical to the rail shell's — the shell class decides the desktop layout.",
    href: "rail.html",
    linkText: "Rail shell"
  }) }}
</section>

